Kubernetes Networking

Kubernetes networking is the system that allows Pods, Services, and external users to communicate with each other.
One of the biggest design goals of Kubernetes is:
Every application should be able to communicate without worrying about where it is running.

Whether a Pod is running on Node 1 or Node 10, communication should work seamlessly.
==============================================================================================================================
Pod Networking

A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes.
Every Pod receives its own unique IP address.
Unlike Docker containers running on the same host, Kubernetes treats each Pod as if it were a separate machine on the network.

This means:

Pod A can directly communicate with Pod B.
No NAT is required between Pods.
Pods can communicate across different nodes.

This is one of the core networking principles of Kubernetes.

Why Pod IPs Are Not Enough

Pods are temporary.

When a Pod crashes, gets deleted, or is recreated during an update, Kubernetes creates a new Pod.
The new Pod usually receives a different IP address.
Applications cannot depend on Pod IPs because they are not permanent.

This creates a problem:

How can applications reliably find each other if IPs keep changing?

The solution is Services.
=======================================================================================================
Service

A Service is a stable network endpoint that sits in front of one or more Pods.
Applications communicate with the Service rather than directly with Pods.
The Service remains constant even if Pods are recreated.

You can think of a Service as a permanent reception desk.
Employees may come and go, but the reception desk always exists.

The Service automatically forwards traffic to healthy Pods.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Service Discovery

In traditional environments, applications often need to know exact server IP addresses.
In Kubernetes, applications discover each other using Services.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ClusterIP Service

ClusterIP is the default Service type.
It exposes an application only inside the Kubernetes cluster.
External users cannot access it directly.
Most internal components use ClusterIP services.

Examples include:

Frontend communicating with backend
Backend communicating with database
Applications communicating with Redis
Microservices communicating with each other

ClusterIP is therefore the most commonly used Service type in Kubernetes.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NodePort Service

NodePort allows external users to access an application.
Kubernetes opens a port on every node.
Traffic arriving on that port is forwarded to the appropriate Service and Pods.

NodePort is useful for:

Learning Kubernetes
Testing applications
Small lab environments

However, it becomes difficult to manage many applications because every application requires a different port.
Load Balancing Inside Kubernetes
A Service does more than provide a stable IP.
It also distributes traffic among multiple Pods.

Suppose an application has three replicas.

When requests arrive at the Service, Kubernetes automatically distributes traffic across all available Pods.

This provides:

Better performance
Higher availability
Fault tolerance

If one Pod fails, traffic is sent to the remaining healthy Pods.
==========================================================================================================
DNS in Kubernetes

Imagine an application needing to connect to a database.
Remembering IP addresses would be difficult and unreliable.

Kubernetes solves this using DNS.

Every Service automatically receives a DNS name.
Applications can communicate using names instead of IP addresses.
This means developers do not need to know where an application is running.
They only need to know the Service name.
DNS is therefore one of the most important features in Kubernetes networking.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CoreDNS

CoreDNS is the DNS server used by Kubernetes.
Whenever an application tries to access a Service name, CoreDNS converts that name into the correct Service IP address.
CoreDNS acts like a phone directory for the cluster.
Without CoreDNS, applications would need to know actual IP addresses.
========================================================================================================================
Network Policies

By default, Kubernetes networking is very open.
Any Pod can communicate with any other Pod.
In production environments this is often too permissive.
Network Policies allow administrators to control which Pods can communicate with each other.

They function similarly to firewall rules.

For example:

Allow frontend Pods to talk to backend Pods.
Allow backend Pods to talk to database Pods.
Prevent frontend Pods from directly accessing databases.

Network Policies improve security and reduce the attack surface.
==================================================================================================================
Ingress

As organizations deploy more applications, exposing each application separately becomes difficult.

Imagine having:

Jenkins
Grafana
SonarQube
ArgoCD

If each application requires a separate NodePort or LoadBalancer, management becomes complicated and expensive.

Ingress solves this problem.

Ingress provides a single entry point into the cluster.
Instead of exposing every application separately, external traffic enters through one place and is then routed to the correct application.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Ingress Routing

Ingress makes routing decisions based on:

Hostnames

Examples:

jenkins.company.com
grafana.company.com
sonarqube.company.com

or

URL Paths

Examples:

company.com/jenkins
company.com/grafana
company.com/sonarqube

This allows many applications to share the same IP address and standard web ports.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Ingress Controller

Ingress itself is only a set of routing rules.
It does not process traffic.

An Ingress Controller is responsible for reading those rules and performing the actual routing.
Without an Ingress Controller, Ingress resources do nothing.

Popular controllers include:

NGINX Ingress Controller
Traefik
HAProxy Ingress

The controller is the actual component handling incoming requests.
=====================================================================================================================================
Real Production Flow

In most companies, traffic flows like this:

User sends request → Ingress Controller receives request → Ingress rules determine destination → Service receives request → Service forwards request → Pod processes request
Instead of remembering an IP address, an application only needs to know the Service name.

This makes applications more portable and resilient.
